  "narrative": {
    "essence": "South Africa returns to the World Cup for the first time since they hosted it in 2010, ending a 16-year absence. Bafana Bafana arrive not as contenders but as a nation rediscovering its football identity — and in Group A, they have nothing to lose.",
    "footballIdentity": "Football in South Africa carries the weight of democracy. The 2010 World Cup, when South Africa became the first African nation to host the tournament, was a transformative national moment. The vuvuzela, the spirit, the flag — Bafana Bafana are tied to a country's post-apartheid self-image. Their return to the global stage in 2026 is as much political redemption as it is sporting achievement.",
    "theMoment": "South Africa qualified for their first World Cup since 2010 after years of underperformance that prompted serious questions about the state of South African football. Coach Hugo Broos, a Belgian with a reputation for rebuilding struggling programs, transformed a dispirited squad into AFCON contenders. The political symbolism of returning to football's biggest stage — especially with South Africa's socioeconomic tensions — makes this more than just sport.",
    "momentType": "historical"
  },
